          "content": "The first thing Mara bought at the estate sale was a kitchen timer shaped like a lemon. It cost fifty cents and had a crack down one side. The woman at the cash box said it ran slow. \u201cBy how much?\u201d Mara asked. \u201cDepends what you\u2019re waiting for,\u201d she said.\n\nAt home Mara set it for ten minutes and put the kettle on. While the water heated she opened a drawer that had been sticking since winter. A takeout menu had wedged itself behind the cutlery tray. On the back her father had written a phone number without a name.\n\nShe dialed. A man answered on the fourth ring, out of breath. She apologized and said she was trying to find out whose number this was. \u201cWell,\u201d he said, \u201cyou\u2019ve found out it\u2019s mine.\u201d Then he asked her to wait while he let the old dog back into the house.\n\nWhen he came back, he knew her father\u2019s name. They had played chess on Thursdays at the library until the library got rid of its chess tables. \u201cHe always brought an orange,\u201d the man said. \u201cHe could peel it in one piece. Did you know he could do that, too?\u201d\n\nMara hadn\u2019t known. She had never seen him eat an orange. She told the man this and heard him sit down; the chair made a little scrape. The timer went off. Mara cupped her hand over the lemon until it stopped rattling, then asked who usually won on Thursday afternoons.",
